#!/bin/sh
# modules-ok — exit 0 when this kernel's modules can actually be loaded.
#
# Alpine mounts the modloop squashfs at /.modloop and points /lib/modules
# into it. USB flash is the most failure-prone part of this whole design, and
# a rotting stick (or a torn modloop write) leaves the box RUNNING with no
# loadable modules at all:
#
#   - no zram, on a box whose root filesystem IS RAM
#   - no af_packet, so udhcpc cannot open its socket and the box never gets
#     an address — it looks DEAD on the network rather than degraded
#   - no filesystem driver that is not already built into the kernel, so a
#     data disk can silently fail to mount
#
# Alpine does not treat a failed modloop as fatal and boots on to a login
# prompt, starting every service. Nothing else notices, so MountNAS has to be
# the one that says it out loud. See 'nas status', gen-issue and the
# supervisor, which all ask this.
#
# modules.dep is the index modprobe itself reads, so its absence IS the
# failure however it was caused. Deliberately NOT 'mountpoint -q /.modloop':
# 'nas upgrade' (_free_modloop) copies the modules to RAM and unmounts the
# modloop ON PURPOSE, and such a box is healthy until it reboots — a mount
# test would cry wolf after every single upgrade.
#
# One stat, no device I/O, so this is safe on the fast path that must never
# spin up a sleeping disk.
[ -f "/lib/modules/$(uname -r)/modules.dep" ]
